export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="models">

          <h3>Free models inside of Vector</h3>
          <p>
            The upcoming release adds a section for approved OpenRouter free models when the service is enabled.
            Sign in to Vector for a shared allowance, or choose Connect OpenRouter to use your own account directly.
            Each model identifies its source: Shared Vector allowance or Your OpenRouter account.
            When both offer the same model, your account takes precedence. The section is hidden when the service is off.
          </p>
          <p>
            Uses your own free OpenRouter account: 50 requests a day, or 1,000 if you've ever added $10 of OpenRouter credits.
            OpenRouter supplies this free access; Vector does not charge for, provide, or pay for these tokens.
            When the shared allowance is exhausted, Vector shows its expected reset time and lets you connect OpenRouter
            to continue the same conversation. Workspace subscription terms are unchanged.
          </p>
          <p>
            Prompts are processed by OpenRouter and the selected model provider. Retention and training policies vary
            by provider; review those policies before sending confidential code. This describes upcoming functionality,
            not an announcement that the shared service is enabled in production.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            The upcoming release no longer loads the former third-party hosted providers, even with an API key.
            Choose another provider before starting a task. Native ChatGPT, Copilot, xAI, GitLab OAuth, Poe and DigitalOcean
            sign-in flows are paused pending Vector's own registrations; API-key methods remain where supported.
            External Claude Code, Codex,
            and Cursor runtimes still use your own installed CLIs and their authentication.
          </p>
          <h3>Bring your own key</h3>
          <p>
            Connect a provider in Settings, or run <code>vector providers login</code> in the terminal, then
            select a model in the session composer. Model availability, context limits, pricing, and
            rate limits come from the provider. Vector's usage view tracks the information returned by
            those sessions.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
